    "ss_hero_27_b6HDbY": {
      "type": "ss-hero-27",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_U7heJp": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "<p>Why the Foot Connection Matters</p>",
            "heading_custom": true,
            "heading_font": "roboto_n4",
            "heading_size": 38,
            "heading_size_mobile": 32,
            "heading_height": 120,
            "heading_mt": 0,
            "heading_mt_mobile": 0,
            "heading_color": "#121212"
          }
        },
        "text_iEdF3b": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>Pilates depends on feedback. The foot has to sense, press, articulate, stabilize, and respond.</strong></p>",
            "text_custom": false,
            "text_font": "josefin_sans_n4",
            "text_size": 18,
            "text_size_mobile": 16,
            "text_height": 150,
            "text_mt": 16,
            "text_mt_mobile": 12,
            "text_color": "#121212"
          }
        },
        "text_bE9jyF": {
          "type": "text",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p><strong>A Bridge Between Original Pilates Principles and Modern Studio Fitness</strong></p>",
            "text_custom": false,
            "text_font": "josefin_sans_n4",
            "text_size": 18,
            "text_size_mobile": 16,
            "text_height": 150,
            "text_mt": 16,
            "text_mt_mobile": 12,
            "text_color": "#121212"
          }
        },
        "text_7AhGCD": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>A traditional grip sock that slips, shifts, bunches, or traps moisture can dull the connection between the foot and the work. When that happens, your focus moves away from form, strength, breath, and control and toward correcting your footing.<br/><br/></p><p>It is like trying to hold a plank while a towel is sliding under your foot. You can still do the move, but your body is compensating instead of fully engaging.<br/><br/></p><p>That is the part I believe Joseph Pilates, and any serious barre or Pilates practitioner, would understand immediately. <strong>A practice built on control, precision, stability, and connection should not be compromised by what is happening beneath your feet.<br/></strong><br/></p><p>And while socks became common in studios for hygiene and studio rules, a sock alone does not make a practice clean. Traditional fabric socks can still absorb sweat, hold moisture, and carry bacteria back onto the mat or machine. Clean feet, clean floors, clean mats, wiped down equipment, and a consistent studio process are what actually create a clean environment.<br/><br/></p><p>For anyone who prefers a sock layer, has cold feet, or attends a studio that requires socks, Performance Skins can be worn with a very thin sock underneath. We recommend the thinnest option possible, ideally toe free or fitted, so you still feel the grip, stability, and connection Barreletics was designed to create.<br/><br/></p><p><strong>The Pilates sock era is over. A better way to grip is here.</strong></p>",
            "text_custom": false,
            "text_font": "josefin_sans_n4",
            "text_size": 16,
            "text_size_mobile": 16,
            "text_height": 150,
            "text_mt": 16,
            "text_mt_mobile": 12,
            "text_color": "#121212"
          }
        },
        "text_geKpU4": {
          "type": "text",
          "disabled": true,
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Joseph Pilates developed his method, originally called Contrology, around breath, concentration, control, precision, alignment, flow, and full body awareness.<br/><br/></p><p>He also created apparatus to support that method, including the Reformer, Cadillac, Wunda Chair, Ladder Barrel, Spine Corrector, Foot Corrector, Toe Corrector, and Ped O Pul.<br/><br/></p><p>The feet were always part of the system.</p><p>In Pilates, the foot connects the body to the floor, mat, Reformer, Cadillac, Wunda Chair, Megaformer, and every piece of equipment beneath you. That connection matters.<br/><br/></p><p>Barreletics was designed to bridge the gap between the barefoot style connection of the original practice and the grip, stability, protection, and easy clean function required in today’s studio environment.<br/><br/></p><p><strong>The Pilates sock era is over. A better way to grip is here.<br/><br/></strong></p><p>A traditional grip sock that slips, shifts, bunches, or traps moisture can dull the connection between the foot and the work. When that happens, your focus moves away from form, strength, breath, and control and toward correcting your footing.<br/><br/></p><p>It is like trying to hold a plank while a towel is sliding under your foot. You can still do the move, but your body is compensating instead of fully engaging.<br/><br/></p><p>That is the part I believe Joseph Pilates, and any serious barre or Pilates practitioner, would understand immediately. A practice built on control, precision, stability, and connection should not be compromised by the very thing between your foot and the work.<br/><br/></p><p><strong>The tool beneath you should support the practice, not compromise it.</strong></p>",
