    "17807939354bbe0595": {
      "type": "_blocks",
      "blocks": {
        "ai_gen_block_da9aeb3_bjb9JV": {
          "type": "ai_gen_block_da9aeb3",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Why the Foot Connection Matters",
            "heading_size": 36,
            "heading_spacing": 16,
            "subheading": "This matters because Pilates is built on feedback. The foot has to sense, press, articulate, stabilize, and respond. A traditional grip sock that slips, shifts, bunches, or traps moisture can dull that connection and pull the practitioner out of the work.",
            "subheading_size": 24,
            "subheading_spacing": 16,
            "body_text": "<p>Over time, socks became the standard in many studios for hygiene and studio rules. But a sock alone does not make a practice clean. Traditional fabric socks can still absorb sweat, hold moisture, and carry bacteria back onto the mat or machine. Clean feet, clean floors, clean mats, wiped down equipment, and a consistent studio process are what actually create a clean environment.</p><p>It is common sense. Just like you would not finish dinner and put a dirty plate back in the cabinet, studio equipment should be cleaned after use. Socks do not replace that responsibility.</p><p>For anyone who prefers a sock layer, has cold feet, or attends a studio that requires socks, Performance Skins can be worn with a very thin sock underneath. We recommend the thinnest option possible, ideally toe free or fitted, so you still feel the grip, stability, and connection Barreletics was designed to create.</p><p>It is like trying to hold a plank while a towel is sliding under your foot. You can still do the move, but your focus shifts from form, strength, and control to correcting your footing.</p><p>That is the part I believe Joseph Pilates, and any serious barre or Pilates practitioner, would understand immediately. A practice built on control, precision, stability, and connection should not be compromised by the very thing between your foot and the work.</p><p>Barreletics was designed to preserve the freedom, flexibility, toe engagement, and connection of barefoot style training while adding the grip, stability, confidence, protection, and easy to clean function required in today’s studio environment.</p><p><strong>The tool beneath you should support the practice, not compromise it.</strong></p>",
            "body_size": 16,
            "show_background_placeholder": false,
            "overlay_color": "#000000",
            "overlay_opacity": 0.3,
            "text_color": "#000000",
            "text_alignment": "left",
            "padding_vertical": 60,
